{
  "enabled": true,
  "name": "No emoji in any UI string",
  "description": "A deliberate product decision, not a style preference. The guard is narrowed to Emoji_Presentation=Yes so intentional monochrome glyphs pass - narrow it further if it misfires, never delete it.",
  "version": "1",
  "when": {
    "type": "fileEdited",
    "patterns": [
      "shell/*.py",
      "shell/pages/*.py",
      "chat_hud.py",
      "overlay.py",
      "settings_dialog.py",
      "activation_dialog.py",
      "tray.py",
      "onboarding.py",
      "theme.py",
      "brand.py",
      "annotations.py"
    ]
  },
  "then": {
    "type": "askAgent",
    "prompt": "A UI module changed. Run `TestNoEmojiInTheUi` and confirm it passes.\n\nThis is a product decision, not a lint rule: Nimbus is a teaching tool that professionals leave running all day, and an emoji in a status line is the single fastest way to make it read as a toy. The guard walks the AST over every string literal, skips docstrings, and is narrowed to `Emoji_Presentation=Yes` so that deliberate monochrome glyphs pass.\n\nIf it fires:\n- Replace the emoji with a painted primitive or a monochrome symbol glyph. `titlebar.GlyphButton` is the precedent - the maximise glyph is DRAWN because U+2B1C renders as a solid white block in Segoe UI, and every text substitute fails differently on some fallback.\n- Name the symbol font explicitly rather than leaving it to fallback, the way `theme.SYMBOL_FONT_STACK` does.\n- If you add a new glyph, measure its ink height against the surrounding cap height before shipping it. `RESTART_MARKER` documents that method: an icon-font glyph measured ~40% larger than the letters beside it because an icon font fills the em box while a text character's capitals occupy about 70% of it.\n\nIf the guard misfires on a legitimate glyph, NARROW it. Do not delete or weaken it."
  }
}
